Hi !

I managed to do this by using the public annotations on the affymetrix website (netaffx i believe). Its far away in my memory, but they provide a format that is like..

1 // 2 // 3 // 4 /// 1 // 2 // 3 // 4

This is probably not the best way but it worked for me... Just adapt it to your needs and get the human nettaffx.

Cheers !
Math
McGill University

I used:
# To get the annotation
descriptions  <- read.csv(file="...PATH/RaEx-1_0-st-v1.na29.rn4.transcript.csv",
        header = TRUE, skip=20)
matched.descriptions  <-  match(probes, descriptions[,1])
sign.descriptions <- descriptions[matched.descriptions,]
gene_assign <- sign.descriptions[,8]
gene_assign_split  <- unlist(lapply(gene_assign, function(v)
        {unlist(strsplit(as.character(v), "///"))[[1]]}))
# For the table...
probes  <-  as.character(rownames(sign.matrix))
probe.counts  <-  as.vector(sign.descriptions[,7])
accession  <-  as.vector(unlist(lapply(gene_assign_split, function(v)
        {unlist(strsplit(as.character(v), "//"))[1]})))
gene_symbol  <-  as.vector(unlist(lapply(gene_assign_split, function(v)
        {unlist(strsplit(as.character(v), "//"))[2]})))
gene_title  <-  as.vector(unlist(lapply(gene_assign_split, function(v)
        {unlist(strsplit(as.character(v), "//"))[3]})))
entrez_gene_id  <-  as.vector(unlist(lapply(gene_assign_split, function(v)
        {unlist(strsplit(as.character(v), "//"))[5]})))

